<p>The Havana Club, a longtime basement nightclub, built its success on a reputation for excellent food, service, and atmosphere. Like Bismarck's McKenzie—later Patterson—Hotel, it was a place where many political and business deals, both legal and otherwise, took place. {% person "leo-guon" %}Leo Guon{% endperson %} left the business to his son when he died in November 1961, but the son was less enthusiastic about the food-service industry. Without close oversight, the club's reputation deteriorated. Finances tightened, federal payroll taxes went unpaid, and the Internal Revenue Service closed the club in July 1964.</p>

