    <figcaption>Seven Seas; photograph courtesy of Esther Davis</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Davises needed to distance the business from the Havana Club's declining reputation. They remodeled the interior, adding a miniature waterfall, fish tanks, and koi—brocaded carp—to the waterfall's pool. A contest produced a new name: the Seven Seas.</p>

<p>Area friends helped promote the club, and patrons slowly returned. Its warm atmosphere attracted area businesses for luncheon meetings and other gatherings, and many of the same business and professional customers returned in the evenings. Over four years, Friday and Saturday dinner service grew from 10–12 meals a night to 165–175.</p>

<p>The club's largest events were its annual New Year's Eve parties. Despite the hectic pace of the capacity crowds, the owners shared a midnight champagne toast with the kitchen and service crews in recognition of their work. The Seven Seas remained downtown until fire destroyed the building in 1970.</p>

